/** Fictional manuscript for offline checks and reusable product footage. */
export const LIVING_DESK_MANUSCRIPT =
  "<h2>I. Before the flood</h2><p>I returned to Hollins with my notebook in my coat. I wanted to find out why I remembered the water before I remembered the rain. At the old bridge, Mara Okafor stood beside the gauge. She had tied a blue ribbon around the post to mark the level she remembered from childhood.</p><p>I walked with Mara Okafor to the center of Hollins, past the theater and the neighbor's empty shop. We argue that memory begins in places like this. I wrote the sentence down, then wondered why I had suddenly borrowed a crowd to say something I believed alone.</p><p>The shopkeeper offered bread, milk, and apples. At the next door, a volunteer had gathered boots, coats, and blankets. I carried my notebook, a pencil and matches. My coat was grey. The color had faded in the sun, though I remembered its color as blue. I counted three doors, two windows and 4 steps before the lane disappeared into mud.</p><h2>II. The waterline</h2><p>I followed the wall to Hollis and stopped at the painted line. We believe the river keeps a record. I could see my own reflection below the mark, trembling whenever a truck crossed the bridge. As we have seen, a record is only useful when someone knows how to read it.</p><p>My family and I worked at the shelter that evening. We all carried boxes from the school to the dry upper rooms. I kept my notes in the pocket nearest my chest. The day had become a list of small decisions: where to put the cups, who needed a blanket, when to go home.</p><h2>III. After the water</h2><p>In the morning, Mara Okafor found me beside the bridge. We think the town will remember this differently. I crossed out the borrowed certainty and listened to the sounds around me: the slow scrape of a shovel, water running through a broken gutter, a door that would no longer close.</p><p>I returned to Hollins a week later. The first dry pavement looked unfamiliar. My notebook was still warped at the corners. I opened it at the last clean page and wrote what I could see, leaving enough room for the things I had missed.</p>";

